  "raw_text": "69\n\nSibrin dingkaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto cause to be afraid, to cause dread, to cause terror\n \n/ causar miedo, causar temor, causar\nterror\n\nSibrin dingki rau munaia\n \n(\nfg13\n) (v)\n \n–\n \nto do fear\n-\ncausing violence, to do terrorism\n \n/ hacer violencia que\n\ncausa miedo, hacer terrorismo\n\nSkul dingkaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto put in school, to enroll someone in school\n \n/ poner en la escuela,\n \nmatricular\n \na alguien en la\n\nescuela\n\nYukuwi dingkaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto enter while hiding, to smuggle\n \n/ entrar\n \na\n \nescondid\nas\n,\n \nmeter a escondidas,\n \npasar de\n\ncontrabando\n\nDipasta\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ntrash, litter, garbage\n \n/ basura\n\nDipasta yakabaia pliska\n \n(\nbh73\n)\n \n–\n \ngarbage dump\n \n/ basurero\n\nDiran\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nthirst\n \n/ sed\n\nDiran bla\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nstrong thirst\n \n/ mucha sed\n\nDiran bla pruaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto die of thirst\n \n/ morir de sed\n\nDiran daukaia\n \n(\ndb\n) (v)\n \n–\n \nto be thirsty\n \n/ tener sed\n\n(Note/Nota: This appears to be an old term to say:\n \nto be thirsty.\n \nThe verb\n \nDin ai/mai daukaia\n \nis the common way now.\n\nSee above under\n \nDiaia\n \nfor examples.\n \n/ Este parece ser un viejo término para decir:\n \ntener sed\n. El verbo\n \nDin ai/mai daukaia\n\nes la forma común ahora. Ver arriba bajo\n \nDiaia\n \npara ejemplos.\n)\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\ndb\n-\nAiseya\n \n32:6\n):\n \ndiran dauki nani ba\n \n–\n \nthirsty ones\n \n/ los sedientos\n)\n\n(Ex/Ej(\ndb-Matiu 5:6\n):\n \ndiran dauki\n \n–\n \nthirsting\n \n/ sediento)\n\n(Ex/Ej(\ndb-Jan 4:15\n):\n \nkli diran ai daukbia apia\n \n–\n \nI will not again be thirsty\n \n/ No volveré a tener sed)\n\nDiranka\n \n(\ndb\n-...) (n/s)\n \n–\n \ndrink, beverage\n \n/ bebida\n \ncommonly used?\n\nDirankira\n \n(adj)\n \n–\n \nthirsty\n \n/ sediento\n\n(Note/Nota: This appears to be an old term to say:\n \nto be thirsty.\n \nThe verb\n \nDin ai/mai daukaia\n \nis the common way now.\n\nSee above under\n \nDiaia\n \nfor examples. / Este parece ser un viejo término para decir:\n \ntener sed\n. El verbo\n \nDin ai/mai daukaia\n\nes la forma común ahora. Ver arriba bajo\n \nDiaia\n \npara ejemplos.)\n\nDis\n \n(\nkt,my,bh,lv,fy\n \n; but not in / pero no en:\n \ndict\n,\ndictu\n)\n \n–\n \njust, only, merely\n \n/ solo, solamente\n\n(Note\n/Nota\n:\n \nDis\n \nis also the imperative form of\n \nDiaia\n \n/\n \nDis\n \nes también la forma imperativa de\n \nDiaia\n)\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\nbh28\n):\n \n“\nPyuta” makisa, sakuna dis daiwan lupia kum apia sa\n \n–\n \nIt is called “the snake”, however it is not just a mere animal\n \n/\n\nSe llama \"la serpiente\", sin embargo, no es solo un simple animal)\n\nDisaipil\n \n(\ngf,fy,ll,jl,fg,km\n,dictu\n;\n \na/t\n:\n \nDisaipel\n-\ndict\n,lv,bh,ds,sn\n,\ndictu\n-\nalt\n)\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ndisciple\n \n/ discípulo\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nwqu\n-\n03/20\n):\n \nNahki markisa yawan ba Jisas disaipilka sa?\n \n–\n \nHow do we show that we are Jesus’ disciples?\n \n/\n \n¿Cómo\n\ndemostramos que somos discípulos de Jesús?)\n\nDisaipil daukaia warkka ba\n \n(n\n/s\n)\n \n–\n \nthe\n \nwork to make disciples\n \n/ el trabajo para hacer discípulos\n\nDisaipil nani daukaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto make disciples\n \n/ hacer discípulos\n\nDisang\n \n(\ndb,dictu,dict\n-\nalt\n;\n \nDisan\n-\ndict\n)\n \n(adj)\n \n–\n \nuncultivated (land), fertile (land), first (of a harvest)\n \n/ no cultivada\n \n(tierra),\n\nfértil (tierra), primero (de una cosecha)\n\nDisang m\nâ\n \n–\n \nfirst fruits\n \n/ primeros frutos\n\nDiskaia\n \n–\n \nsee/ver:\n \nMadiskaia\n\nDista\n \n(n/s,adj)\n \n–\n \nshade, shadow, shady\n \n/ sombra\n\n(Construct:\n \nDistika\n)\n\nKasbrika distika\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nshadow of a cloud\n \n/ sombra de una nube\n\nDiwas laya\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ndew\n \n/ rocío\n\nDiwas laya tahwi ba\n \n(\ndb\n) (n/\ns)\n \n–\n \ndewdrop\n \n/ gota de rocío\n\nDrabaia\n \n(v) (v-trans; intrans:\n \nDrawaia\n)\n \n–\n \nto stretch, to extend\n \n/ extender, estirar\n\nDragun\n \n(\ndb,dba,bh,lfb,mwb,sp\n;\n \nDragon\n-dictu,dbta,lrw,sln\n;\n \nDragan\n-\ndict\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \ndragon\n \n/ dragón\n\nDrap\n \n(\ndictu\n)\n \n(1) (\na/t\n:\n \nDrop\n)\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \npassion fruit\n \n/ maracuyá\n\n(Used in\n \nmy\n \nand\n \nlfb\n \nto refer to\n \npomegranates\n \n/ Usado en\n \nmy\n \ny\n \nlfb\n \npara referirme a granadas)\n\nDrap\n \n(2) (a/t:\n \nDrop\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \ndrop, little\n \n/ gota, pequeño\n\n(Note/Nota: from English / de inglés:\n \ndrop\n)\n\nDrap drap\n \n–\n \nlittle by little\n \n/ poco a poco\n\n(Note/Nota: like English / como inglés:\n \ndrop by drop\n)\n\nDrap kum\n \n–\n \na little\n \n/ un poco\n \n(\nS\nee also/\n \nV\ner también:\n \nWira kum\n)\n\nDrapapa\n \n(n/\ns,adj)\n \n–\n \nmud\n, muddy\n \n/\n \nlodo, lodoso",
